        title: 'Packaging a Franchise',
        html: '<p>The Red/Blue dual packaging established a visual system that Pokémon has maintained for nearly three decades. Every subsequent generation of mainline Pokémon games has used version-paired packaging with consistent typography, mascot-forward imagery, and colour differentiation — a packaging identity so consistent that the system is recognisable from across a retail floor without reading any text. The visual language has evolved in typography and art style while retaining the core dual-version structure, making each new generation\'s packaging simultaneously familiar and updated.</p><p>The packaging\'s influence on franchise design is visible in the games themselves: the version split that the dual boxes communicated became a permanent structural element of the mainline series, with each generation introducing version-exclusive Pokémon, version-exclusive story elements, and version-exclusive mascot legendaries. The packaging did not merely present a product — it communicated a system of ongoing engagement that has proven robust enough to sustain commercial viability across nine generations of games. The Red/Blue boxes are, in retrospect, the first expression of a franchise visual language that has proven more durable than any other in gaming history.</p>',
