    longDescription: 'When Sega launched the Genesis in North America in 1989, it packaged the software in a hinged black plastic case with a clear outer sleeve holding a printed paper insert, and clips inside to retain the cartridge and manual. The proportions were close to a VHS tape, which was not an accident: the video rental shelf was the retail furniture of the late 1980s, and a game that stood on a shelf like a film sat naturally in the stores and the living rooms Sega was targeting.\n\nThe decision produced a genuinely divided identity for the same games across regions. European Mega Drive releases came in card boxes, and Japanese releases used their own hard plastic case format with distinctive spine artwork; a European and an American copy of the same cartridge are visually unrelated objects. For collectors this makes the Mega Drive one of the more region-conscious platforms to assemble — a "complete" library means choosing which of three packaging traditions you are collecting.\n\nThe format\'s durability turned out to be its lasting advantage. Cardboard crushes, tears at the corners, and rubs at the edges; plastic does not. A Genesis clamshell that spent a decade in a cupboard usually still closes, still holds its manual, and still presents its insert. What fails instead is the plastic itself — hinges become brittle and snap, and the clear sleeve yellows and clouds with age and light. The failure modes are different from those of cardboard, and considerably more forgiving, which is a large part of why complete Genesis software is markedly less scarce and less expensive than its cardboard-boxed contemporaries on Nintendo hardware.',
