        title: 'What Survives, and Why',
        html: '<p>Collector markets are shaped less by how many copies were sold than by how many survived in presentable condition, and packaging material is the single largest determinant of that. Cardboard fails progressively and irreversibly under ordinary domestic conditions: shelf pressure crushes corners, humidity warps panels, and a box removed and replaced a hundred times shows every one of those hundred times along its edges.</p><p>Plastic simply does not degrade that way. The clamshell absorbs handling that would destroy a card box, and its contents — the manual especially — are held flat and protected rather than loose. The consequence is visible in prices: complete-in-box Genesis titles are widely available at modest cost, while the equivalent SNES titles command substantial premiums for the box alone. Two libraries of comparable size and comparable sales diverged in value chiefly because one was packed in plastic and the other in card.</p><p>The plastic has its own decay, and collectors know its vocabulary well. Hinges crack from age and cold; the clear sleeve yellows under sunlight and clouds with handling; the internal clips that hold the cartridge snap and leave the contents rattling. None of these are as terminal as a crushed cardboard corner, but a case with intact hinges and a clear sleeve is meaningfully harder to find than the raw survival numbers suggest.</p>',
