    longDescription: 'North American Super Nintendo software shipped in a printed cardboard box containing an internal tray or insert that held the cartridge in place and kept the manual flat. The design was inherited directly from the NES, and it carried Nintendo\'s trade dress — the consistent typography, the layout conventions, the Official Nintendo Seal of Quality — which had been introduced after the 1983 crash specifically to make the platform\'s software look reliable on a shelf.\n\nAs packaging it was cheap, printable in colour to a high standard, and completely disposable, which is exactly what happened to it. The cartridge is the durable object: it survives being dropped, stacked, blown into and left on a floor for a decade. The box protects nothing that needs protecting once the game is home, takes up shelf space, and gets thrown out. A generation of owners kept the carts and binned the boxes, and the survival rate of the two components diverged by an order of magnitude.\n\nThe consequences shape the retro market today. For most SNES titles the loose cartridge is inexpensive and easy to find, while a complete-in-box copy in presentable condition costs a large multiple of it — the premium is for cardboard, not software. This is what produced the grading industry that now surrounds the format, with third-party services sealing boxes in acrylic and assigning numeric condition scores, and the corresponding vocabulary of shelf wear, corner crush and seam splits that buyers scrutinise in photographs. The box became the collectible and the game became the accessory.',
