        title: 'Grading, Sealing and the Inversion of Value',
        html: '<p>As prices for complete copies rose, a third-party grading industry grew around the format, borrowing wholesale from comics and sports cards: a box is submitted, assessed against a condition rubric, assigned a numeric grade, and sealed in an acrylic case. The vocabulary buyers now use — shelf wear along the bottom edge, corner crush, seam splits, sun fade on the spine — is the vocabulary of print collecting applied to video games.</p><p>Sealing has a peculiar consequence: a graded, encased copy cannot be played without destroying the grade. The object has been fully separated from its function. What is being bought and stored is a printed cardboard container in a state of preservation, and the software it was manufactured to sell is present only as provenance.</p><p>Whether this is preservation or speculation is genuinely contested, and both readings have merit. Grading has demonstrably kept boxes in circulation and in condition that would otherwise have degraded, and it has produced a public record of what these objects looked like. It has also made the physical history of the medium considerably more expensive to hold, and pushed a large amount of it into storage rather than onto shelves where anyone can see it.</p>',
