    longDescription: 'A great many Japanese console releases packaged in hard plastic cases — PC Engine HuCards, Mega Drive cartridges, and later the PlayStation and Saturn CD libraries — shipped with a spine card: a separate strip of printed card, folded and slipped into the case behind the main insert, so that its edge formed the visible spine. It carried the title logo, publisher branding, the retail price and the barcode, and it is a close relative of the obi strip that wraps Japanese books and records.\n\nIts function was retail rather than domestic. Standing in a rack, a game is seen edge-on, so the spine is the only surface doing any selling; a separately printed strip let a publisher put the barcode and price somewhere they could be re-printed for a price change or a budget re-release without redoing the main artwork. Once the game was home, the strip was a loose piece of card with a barcode on it, and the overwhelming majority of them went into the bin within minutes of purchase.\n\nThe result is a collector market in which the presence of the spine card is one of the strongest single determinants of price. Two copies of the same Japanese release, in the same condition, with the same manual and disc, can differ substantially in value on the strength of a strip of card a few millimetres wide. It is the clearest instance in the medium of value attaching to the most disposable component in the package — and, for the same reason, an unusually reliable signal that a copy has been handled carefully, since keeping the strip requires deliberate intent from the very first day.',
