        title: 'Why the Strip Existed',
        html: '<p>The spine card is a solution to a shelving problem. Japanese retail packed software into racks where individual copies were visible only edge-on, which makes the spine the single most valuable printed surface on the package — the only one that competes for attention at the moment of browsing. Printing it separately from the main insert meant it could carry the volatile information: the price, the barcode, and any promotional flash about a bundled item or a re-release.</p><p>That separation had a practical payoff. A publisher cutting the price of a title, or issuing it under a budget label, could print a new strip and leave the expensive main artwork untouched. The same logic governs the obi on a Japanese book, where the wrap carries the marketing copy and the review quotes while the jacket underneath stays clean — and, as with books, the wrap was understood by everyone involved to be temporary.</p>',
