        title: 'The Economics of a Discarded Strip',
        html: '<p>Scarcity in collector markets is produced by survival rates, not production numbers, and the spine card has the worst survival rate of any component in the package. The manual is bound and sits in the case; the insert is the artwork and stays; the disc or cartridge is the product. The strip is a loose piece of card with a price on it that falls out when the case is first opened, and it was thrown away by design-conscious owners and careless ones alike.</p><p>Consequently the price gap between a Japanese copy with its strip and one without is frequently large, and disproportionate to any reasonable account of what the strip is. Listings foreground it, buyers ask for photographs of it specifically, and its absence is disclosed as a defect in the way a missing manual would be.</p><p>There is a defensible logic underneath the apparent absurdity. An owner who still has the strip almost certainly kept the case closed, stored it out of sunlight, and treated the whole package as an object worth preserving from the day they bought it — behaviour that is not retrospectively achievable. The strip is not merely rare; it is evidence of thirty years of care, which is why the market treats a few millimetres of printed card as the most informative thing in the box.</p>',
